WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.833
Welcome to Lecture 11.

00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:07.963
A sentence is not simply a set of words, and a time series is not a bag of measurements.

00:00:07.963 --> 00:00:11.097
Order changes meaning and predictive context.

00:00:11.097 --> 00:00:20.711
The lecture introduces time-series forecasting, speech recognition, machine translation, image captioning, video and biological sequences.

00:00:20.711 --> 00:00:25.517
These examples require different mappings between inputs and outputs.

00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:33.180
Before choosing a model, specify what information is available and what the system must predict at each stage.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.502
A one-to-one model might classify one image.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:42.378
A one-to-many model might generate several caption words from one image representation.

00:00:42.378 --> 00:00:48.255
A many-to-one model consumes a sequence and predicts a single label, such as sentiment.

00:00:48.255 --> 00:00:56.698
Many-to-many models may produce a label for each input time step, or translate one sequence into a different-length sequence.

00:00:56.698 --> 00:01:01.156
The aligned and unaligned cases require different output handling.

00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:07.370
A recurrent cell is a building block, not by itself a complete specification of these tasks.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:14.400
Recurrent networks address variable-length inputs by updating an internal state repeatedly with shared parameters.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.139
The same cell can process different numbers of steps, although training and deployment still have practical limits.

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:27.175
It is the state update, not the mere presence of a loop in program code, that defines this computation.

00:01:27.175 --> 00:01:33.914
In this first part we calculate the recurrence and then connect it to the lecture's character-level language model.

00:01:33.914 --> 00:01:37.840
The later parts explain training through time, gates and attention.

00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:43.767
A basic recurrent cell combines the previous hidden state with the current input.

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:48.901
In the familiar tanh model, separate weight matrices transform those two vectors.

00:01:48.901 --> 00:01:54.733
Their contributions and a bias are added, then the activation produces the new hidden state.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.599
An output projection maps that state to task-specific scores.

00:01:58.599 --> 00:02:04.494
The slides omit some biases for simplicity; we include them explicitly in the implementation.

00:02:04.494 --> 00:02:08.233
Every dimension must agree for these additions to be valid.

00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:13.139
Unroll the recurrence across time to obtain a computational graph.

00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:19.766
The drawing contains several cell boxes, but all refer to the same recurrent parameter matrices.

00:02:19.766 --> 00:02:24.667
Each step has different input values and a different hidden activation.

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:27.981
If a model has hidden width H and input width D,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:33.227
the recurrent matrix has shape H by H and the input matrix has shape H by D.

00:02:33.227 --> 00:02:37.783
Sequence length does not multiply the number of recurrent weights.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:42.942
The hidden state summarizes the observed prefix in a fixed-size vector.

00:02:42.942 --> 00:02:47.412
It is not a guaranteed lossless memory of everything seen earlier.

00:02:47.412 --> 00:02:52.899
To calculate the 3rd state, we must first calculate the 2nd, which needs the 1st.

00:02:52.899 --> 00:02:59.536
Different sequences can be batched together, but the time dependency within each sequence remains.

00:02:59.536 --> 00:03:04.752
An initial state is often 0, though other initial-state choices are possible.

00:03:04.752 --> 00:03:10.373
At inference, remember whether a state should be reset between unrelated sequences.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:16.662
To make the state dependency concrete, use a one-number teaching model.

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:23.165
The new state is the hyperbolic tangent of 0.5 the old state plus the current input,

00:03:23.165 --> 00:03:24.248
starting at 0.

00:03:24.248 --> 00:03:26.416
Feed the inputs 1, 0 and -1.

00:03:26.416 --> 00:03:31.369
The first step applies tanh to 1, giving approximately 0.761594.

00:03:31.369 --> 00:03:39.807
This simple model is not trained and has no output head; its purpose is to expose the hidden-state mechanism.

00:03:40.157 --> 00:03:45.761
At the 2nd step, the input is 0 but the state is not reset.

00:03:45.761 --> 00:03:49.465
0.5 the previous state is about 0.3808,

00:03:49.465 --> 00:03:54.024
so the new state becomes approximately 0.363399.

00:03:54.024 --> 00:03:56.019
The last input is -1.

00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:00.578
Adding it to 0.5 that state, then applying tanh,

00:04:00.578 --> 00:04:03.713
produces approximately -0.674144.

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:10.077
Calculations use full precision even though the display is rounded.

00:04:10.427 --> 00:04:13.007
Reverse the inputs to -1, 0 and 1.

00:04:13.007 --> 00:04:18.470
By symmetry of this specific model, the intermediate states change signs

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.582
and the final state is positive 0.674144.

00:04:21.582 --> 00:04:31.067
This demonstrates order sensitivity, not a claim that every input permutation must always give a distinct state in every RNN.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:36.151
Two different histories can collide in a compressed representation.

00:04:36.151 --> 00:04:44.347
The page lets you step through the update and inspect which previous state actually enters each calculation.

00:04:45.134 --> 00:04:51.931
The lecture's character example uses the training word 'hello' and vocabulary h, e, l, o.

00:04:51.931 --> 00:04:57.124
Inputs h, e, l, l are paired with next-character targets e, l, l, o.

00:04:57.124 --> 00:05:00.408
A one-hot vector identifies each character.

00:05:00.408 --> 00:05:06.594
With a one-hot input, multiplying by the input matrix selects one of its columns.

00:05:06.594 --> 00:05:12.246
A learned embedding is a more compact alternative for a larger vocabulary.

00:05:12.246 --> 00:05:20.494
The recurrent state must also distinguish the two appearances of the letter 'l' by their different prefixes.

00:05:20.844 --> 00:05:25.967
Project each hidden state to 4 logits and apply softmax over the vocabulary.

00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:30.012
The loss at a step is minus the logarithm of the probability

00:05:30.012 --> 00:05:32.641
assigned to the correct next character.

00:05:32.641 --> 00:05:36.416
Sum or average those losses with an explicit convention.

00:05:36.416 --> 00:05:41.135
Our downloadable training lab uses the average over the 4 transitions.

00:05:41.135 --> 00:05:46.460
It computes gradients through all 4 steps and records an actual training trace.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:53.404
The trace is a deterministic small experiment, not a simulation of results from a large language model.

00:05:53.754 --> 00:05:59.735
During training on known sequences, each next input comes from the observed training data,

00:05:59.735 --> 00:06:02.526
even if the previous prediction was wrong.

00:06:02.526 --> 00:06:07.908
During generation, a selected or sampled character is fed back as the next input.

00:06:07.908 --> 00:06:09.968
These are different data flows.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:15.418
A model memorizing 'hello' has not learned English or demonstrated generalization.

00:06:15.418 --> 00:06:17.810
Use held-out data for that question.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.864
In the lab, inspect how probability and cross-entropy change,

00:06:21.864 --> 00:06:27.114
and compare the greedy predicted string with the individual step distributions.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.161
Pause and predict.

00:06:29.161 --> 00:06:34.136
Our training word has two consecutive inputs containing the letter 'l'.

00:06:34.136 --> 00:06:42.403
If the input vector is the same at those positions, must the predicted next-character distributions also be identical?

00:06:42.403 --> 00:06:47.098
Draw the previous hidden state entering each cell before answering.

00:06:47.098 --> 00:06:53.333
Then consider an implementation that resets the hidden state to 0 before every character.

00:06:53.333 --> 00:07:01.180
What contextual information would be lost, and which training transitions would become difficult to distinguish?

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:09.737
The distributions need not be identical because each state combines the current character with a different prefix state.

00:07:09.737 --> 00:07:13.473
Resetting state at every character removes that context;

00:07:13.473 --> 00:07:21.813
a predictor based only on the current letter 'l' cannot distinguish the two desired following letters solely from that input.

00:07:21.813 --> 00:07:24.616
It may instead learn a mixed distribution.

00:07:24.616 --> 00:07:34.490
The exercise is to compare these two models on the tiny training sequence, without interpreting training-set memorization as language understanding.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:35.825
Keep 3 lessons.

00:07:35.825 --> 00:07:39.766
Specify the input and output structure of the sequence task.

00:07:39.766 --> 00:07:44.955
Distinguish parameter sharing from hidden-state values that change across time.

00:07:44.955 --> 00:07:49.355
A language-model head predicts a distribution over the next symbol,

00:07:49.355 --> 00:07:51.982
not an entire sentence in one operation.

00:07:51.982 --> 00:07:54.609
Next we differentiate the unrolled graph

00:07:54.609 --> 00:07:58.944
and examine why long sequences cause memory and gradient problems.

00:07:58.944 --> 00:08:04.920
We will then derive the LSTM and GRU mechanisms that the optional lecture slides introduce.

